[Black "Example 311- Larsen- B. vs. Keres- P.- San Antonio 1972"]
[Result "0-1"]
[FEN "3q4/5p1k/2Q2Pp1/7p/7P/6P1/5PK1/8 w - - 0 1"]

 { Black is a pawn down, but the reduction of the battle to one single flank and White's exposed f6-pawn makes a draw quite likely. However, the game soon took an unexpected turn. } 1. { -KEY- } Qc3 ( { a) Seeing that } 1. Kf3 Qd3 2. Kf4 Qd4 ) 
 ( { b) Or } 1. f3 Qd2 2. Kh3 Qe2 { leads to a perpetual, Larsen moves his queen a bit closer to the kingside. } ) 
Qd5+ 2. f3 Qa2+ 3. Kh3 Qb1 { The threat of mate forces White's king to retreat. } 4. Kg2 Qa2+ 5. Kf1 Qa6+ $1 ( { It is possible that } 5... Qh2 6. Qc7 Qh1 7. Kf2 Qh2 8. Ke3 Qg1 { ends in a perpetual, but the text is simpler. } ) 
6. Ke1 Qe6+ 7. Kf2 ( { Thanks to the previous switch of angle for his queen, Black could now meet } 7. Kd2 $2 { with } Qd6 ) 
Qa2+ 8. Kg1 Qb1+ 9. Kg2 Qa2+ 10. Kh3 Qb1 11. g4 { The last resource, trying to clear some space on the kingside. } Qh1+ 12. Kg3 Qg1+ 13. Kf4 $6 Qh2+ { While White's 13th move was rather optimistic - he should rather have contented himself with the perpetual - his next one is suicidal. } 14. Kg5 $4 { A fatal location for the king, where it will be subject to different mating patterns. } ( { After } 14. Ke4 Qxh4 { Black recovers the pawn with a slight edge, but this was clearly White's least of evils - Keres. } ) 
Qg3 $1 ( { All of a sudden there is no good reply to the threat of } 15... hxg4 ) 
15. Qe3 ( 15. Qc6 $2 { runs into } Qe5 ) 
hxg4 16. Qf4 Qxf3 17. Qxg4 Qe3+ ( { After } 17... Qxg4 18. Kxg4 Kh6 19. Kh3 g5 20. hxg5 Kxg5 21. Kg3 Kxf6 22. Kf4 { White draws. With the text Black continues playing for a win, exploiting the exposed enemy king on g5. } ) 
18. Qf4 Qe2 $1 19. Qg3 ( { Black threatened } 19. Qg3 Qh5 { mate; } ) 
 ( { and } 19. Qg4 $2 { obviously failed to } Qe5 ) 
Qb5+ 20. Kf4 Qf5+ 21. Ke3 Qxf6 { Quite incredibly, White is already on the verge of losing - his h4-pawn is exposed and his king can't get much closer to it. } 22. Qg5 Qf1 23. Qg4 Qe1+ $1 24. Kd3 ( { The more desirable } 24. Kf3 { or } 24. Kf4 { would let Black exchange queens, thus reaching a winning pawn ending. } ) 
Qe6 25. Qf4 Kg7 26. Qd4+ f6 27. Qb4 ( { Or } 27. Qf4 Qe5 28. Qg4 Kh6 { - Keres. } ) 
Qf5+ 28. Ke2 Kh6 $1 { Black is finally ready to attack the h-pawn. } 29. Ke1 ( { After } 29. Qf8 Kh5 30. Qh8 Kg4 31. Qh6 Qh5 $1 { Black also wins - Keres. } ) 
Kh5 30. Qc4 Qg4 31. Qc5+ Kxh4 ( { There followed } 31... Kxh4 32. Qe7 Qf5 33. Qb4 Kh5 34. Qc4 g5 35. Qf7 Kh4 36. Qf8 Kg3 37. Qa3 Qf3 38. Qd6 Kg2 39. Qd2 Kh3 40. Qd7 f5 41. Qg7 g4 42. Qh8 Kg3 43. Qe5 f4 44. Qb8 Qe3 45. Kd1 Kg2 { and White resigned. } ) 

 0-1

